 a benefit**\n- A [2025 network meta-analysis of 56 studies](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13668-025-00684-7) found that modified alternate-day fasting lowered systolic BP by about 7.2 mmHg and diastolic BP by about 4.7 mmHg compared with usual diets. It rated the certainty of this evidence as high.\n- A [2026 BMJ-style network meta-analysis of time-restricted eating (TRE)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12829361) found that self-selected TRE beat usual diets for systolic BP, body weight, fat mass and triglycerides.\n- A [recent review of TRE](https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/ajpcell.00766.2025) describes the most consistent finding as a drop of roughly 4/2 mmHg.\n- A [randomized trial comparing 16:8 with 5:2 fasting](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/oby.70322) saw systolic BP fall by about 6.7 mmHg and diastolic BP by about 1.9 mmHg in both groups. The two regimens did not differ from each other.\n\n**Studies that found little or no effect**\n- A [2024 meta-analysis of TRE](https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6643/16/21/3700) found no significant change in systolic BP (−0.2 mmHg) or diastolic BP (+0.5 mmHg). Heterogeneity between studies was high.\n- A [meta-analysis in overweight and obese women](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/nutrition/articles/10.3389/fnut.2025.1664412/full) found no significant effect of TRE on either systolic or diastolic BP.\n- A [Cochrane review of 22 trials](https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/intermittent-fasting-offers-no-clear-advantage-2026a10005s7), as reported by Medscape, found IF no better than conventional diets for weight loss in people with overweight or obesity. Experts quoted there called the included studies \"extremely heterogeneous.\"\n\n**Overall**\n- **Weight loss may explain much of the effect.** Reductions in BP often track weight loss. When IF is compared with calorie restriction that produces similar weight loss, it shows no clear advantage. One [2025 comparison](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-32673-9) also noted that meta-analyses of 5:2 fasting found BP reductions similar to calorie restriction.\n- **Protocol type may matter.** Alternate-day fasting seems to produce larger BP drops than TRE, which gives small or inconsistent changes.\n- **The evidence has limits.** Most trials are short, from about 4 to 12 weeks, and the studies vary widely in design. Long-term effects on BP and cardiovascular outcomes are unclear.\n- **Check with a clinician first.** This applies especially if you take BP or diabetes medication, because fasting can change how much you need."}]},"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1112,"cache_read_input_tokens":12633,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2156},"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","refusal_category":null}]}
